<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Chen</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 BCE – 479 BCE (approx.) · capital Wanqiu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Zhou state on the Huai plain whose rulers claimed descent from the sage-king Shun, a frequent victim of Chu whose exiled branch later seized the throne of Qi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu of Zhou</a> enfeoffed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-hu-of-chen">Gui Man</a>, Duke Hu of Chen, a descendant of the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shun-emperor">Emperor Shun</a>, at Wanqiu (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaiyang-district">Huaiyang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>), and gave him his eldest daughter in marriage. Chen thus combined ancient lineage with close ties to the royal house.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chen sat on the open plain between the northern states and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a>, and switched allegiance repeatedly. Its court was notorious for intrigue: Duke Ling was killed in 599 BCE by Xia Zhengshu, prompting a Chu intervention. In 672 BCE Prince Wan of Chen fled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi</a>, where his descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> clan, rose to power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> was famously stranded between Chen and Cai.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Chu occupied Chen twice before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-hui-of-chu">King Hui of Chu</a> annexed it for good in 479 BCE. Its capital became an important Chu city, and in 278 BCE served as the Chu capital after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ying-chu">Ying</a> fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> lineage descended from Chen ruled Qi until 221 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-chen.jpg" alt="State of Chen" width="320" height="217"><figcaption>Image: Yug · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_plain_5c._BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1046 BCE – 479 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wanqiu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duke Hu of Chen, Duke Ling of Chen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chen (Zhou state)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-762&amp;focus=state-of-chen" aria-label="State of Chen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7611 954 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Chen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7931 1210L7768 1193M7931 1210L7909 1324"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1193h0M7909 1324h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7931 1210h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7931 1210h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-762&amp;focus=state-of-chen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

